    "seo": {
      "title": "Service Booking Platforms in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How appointment and accommodation platforms organize service discovery, reservations, and cancellation, with examples serving Iran."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "appointments-and-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From finding a provider to reserving capacity",
        "content": "A booking platform makes a service searchable and gives customers a way to request or reserve it. The distinction between booking and delivery matters: reserving a medical appointment does not itself provide a consultation, just as arranging accommodation does not make the intermediary the hotel operator. The inspected examples show these activities presented through different interfaces and contractual arrangements. [s1][s2]\n\nDoctoreto, an Iranian medical platform, separates in-person appointment booking from online consultation. Its Persian-language homepage lets visitors search by doctor, specialty, illness, treatment center, or service and select a city. It also presents telephone, video, and text consultations, alongside medical-center and laboratory services. These are descriptions of the platform's advertised functions; they do not establish that every listed provider offers every consultation format. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "local-availability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Availability is local and service-specific",
        "content": "Doctoreto provides city links including Tehran, Shiraz, Isfahan, Mashhad, Tabriz, and Zahedan. This illustrates a practical feature of appointment marketplaces: users search a national interface, but the service they can actually receive depends on a particular place and provider. A city directory is evidence of geographic organization, rather than proof of equal appointment availability across regions. [s1]\n\nThe homepage also displays cumulative activity and coverage claims. Those figures are excluded here because an undated counter cannot establish annual bookings, unique patients, or a comparable historical growth rate. Establishing those measures would require dated releases and definitions explaining how cancellations, repeated users, and different service types are counted. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "booking-conditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The conditions attached to a reservation",
        "content": "Accommodation booking demonstrates why the reservation's terms are part of the service. ToIran's published terms distinguish the platform from accommodation providers and state that cancellation and no-show policies depend on the relevant property. They also explain that changing a reservation may be treated as cancelling it, and that some offers cannot be changed or refunded. These are that platform's contractual statements, rather than a description of a uniform rule applying to all Iranian bookings. [s2]\n\nDigibilit's hotel-cancellation guidance similarly says that hotels determine cancellation and modification conditions. It explicitly distinguishes cancellation of an event from cancellation of a hotel reservation: one does not automatically refund the other. The example shows how a customer journey can involve several separate commitments even when the purchases serve the same trip. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "understanding-the-category",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What these examples establish",
        "content": "Together, the sources show a category organized around matching demand with provider availability, with different arrangements for clinical appointments and accommodation. They support examining what is reserved, who delivers it, and which conditions govern changes. They do not establish comparative service quality, market leadership, or successful fulfillment rates. Understanding those outcomes requires evidence beyond the booking interface and the platform's own terms. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
